44
00:21:50,040 --> 00:21:52,480
indicating that it's a dilemma of a plurality

45
00:21:52,480 --> 00:21:54,480
of plural prisoners.

46
00:21:54,480 --> 00:21:56,160
It's not a dilemma of a single prisoner.

47
00:21:56,160 --> 00:21:59,240
It's a dilemma of a group of prisoners.

48
00:21:59,240 --> 00:22:02,480
So a standard representation is something like this.

49
00:22:02,480 --> 00:22:05,880
So you've got two people who can either cooperate

50
00:22:05,880 --> 00:22:07,480
with each other or don't.

51
00:22:07,480 --> 00:22:10,800
They can either cooperate with each other or don't.

52
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:15,080
And then, of course, if they both cooperate with each other,

53
00:22:15,080 --> 00:22:17,640
they both get a good payoff.

54
00:22:17,640 --> 00:22:19,560
If neither cooperates with each other,

55
00:22:19,560 --> 00:22:21,640
they both get a bad payoff.

56
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:24,360
But then if one cooperates and one doesn't,

57
00:22:24,360 --> 00:22:26,880
the non-cooperator gets the best payoff,

58
00:22:26,880 --> 00:22:28,760
and the person that did cooperate

59
00:22:28,760 --> 00:22:30,240
gets the worst payoff.

60
00:22:30,240 --> 00:22:34,080
So it would be something like 0, 6, and 6, 0.

61
00:22:34,080 --> 00:22:38,880
So this is the classic setup of the prisoner's dilemma here.

62
00:22:38,880 --> 00:22:43,040
So notice that here, both are better off than there.

63
00:22:43,040 --> 00:22:48,600
OK, all right, so if you end up here, you say, gee,

64
00:22:48,600 --> 00:22:50,000
why couldn't we have been there?

65
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,840
And yet, these two diagonal ones are

66
00:22:52,840 --> 00:22:54,960
what are generally called the temptation payoffs.

67
00:22:54,960 --> 00:22:57,840
These are ones that give a temptation to each player

68
00:22:57,840 --> 00:23:00,160
to not cooperate.

69
00:23:00,160 --> 00:23:03,080
Now, this is a game that is standardly

70
00:23:03,080 --> 00:23:04,200
solved by strict dominance.

71
00:23:04,200 --> 00:23:06,280
I think we talked about it a bit already.

72
00:23:06,280 --> 00:23:10,560
So player one is better off cooperating when?

73
00:23:10,560 --> 00:23:13,040
This is only, we only play the game once,

74
00:23:13,040 --> 00:23:14,360
you never see the person again.

75
00:23:14,360 --> 00:23:16,000
It's all assumed.

76
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:19,760
So when is player one better off not cooperating?

77
00:23:19,760 --> 00:23:20,720
Always.

78
00:23:20,720 --> 00:23:23,280
When is player two better off not cooperating?

79
00:23:23,280 --> 00:23:24,800
Always, yes.

80
00:23:24,800 --> 00:23:28,200
So if it is always better for both of them to not cooperate,

81
00:23:28,200 --> 00:23:29,800
then the game theoretic prediction is,

82
00:23:29,800 --> 00:23:31,640
there will never be cooperation.

83
00:23:31,640 --> 00:23:33,800
And we'll end up there, even though there

84
00:23:33,800 --> 00:23:37,440
is that payoff right there, right in front of us.

85
00:23:37,440 --> 00:23:39,240
But we don't go there.

86
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:40,520
And why don't we go there?

87
00:23:40,520 --> 00:23:43,560
Because we don't act.

88
00:23:43,560 --> 00:23:44,720
There is no we here.

89
00:23:44,720 --> 00:23:46,320
Instead, there are two individuals

90
00:23:46,320 --> 00:23:49,080
each getting to act separately.

91
00:23:49,080 --> 00:23:52,080
So wouldn't society be better off at 5-5?

92
00:23:52,080 --> 00:23:54,560
Or at least the sub-society.

93
00:23:54,560 --> 00:23:56,560
Wouldn't these two guys be better off at 5-5?

94
00:23:56,560 --> 00:23:57,600
Of course.

95
00:23:57,600 --> 00:23:59,520
But if each chooses individually,

96
00:23:59,520 --> 00:24:02,880
there isn't any way for society to take an action.

97
00:24:02,880 --> 00:24:08,040
And so instead, you get this bad result.

98
00:24:08,040 --> 00:24:10,240
So of course, the classic case of the prisoner's lemma

99
00:24:10,240 --> 00:24:12,600
involves actual prisoners.

100
00:24:12,600 --> 00:24:15,000
So you probably heard the story.

101
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,600
So there's two people arrested for a crime.

102
00:24:17,600 --> 00:24:19,960
And then they are usually in the story.

103
00:24:19,960 --> 00:24:21,680
We say we put them in separate rooms.

104
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:24,480
And then the prosecutor goes to each one and says,

105
00:24:24,480 --> 00:24:30,360
look, you can either go and admit that you can go and,

106
00:24:30,360 --> 00:24:31,920
what's the right word?

107
00:24:31,920 --> 00:24:35,080
You go and rat out your accomplice.

108
00:24:35,080 --> 00:24:38,640
And if you do this, you'll get a reduced sentence.

109
00:24:38,640 --> 00:24:40,880
And I'm offering the same deal over in the other room

110
00:24:40,880 --> 00:24:41,400
right now.

111
00:24:41,400 --> 00:24:43,560
So I just thought you ought to know.

112
00:24:43,560 --> 00:24:45,760
And the prediction is that both people

113
00:24:45,760 --> 00:24:51,360
will rat, will rat, leading to the non-cooperative result.

114
00:24:51,360 --> 00:24:53,840
So there actually is a murder mystery called Murder

115
00:24:53,840 --> 00:24:58,360
at the Mergin, written by under the pseudonym Marshall

116
00:24:58,360 --> 00:24:59,560
Jevons.

117
00:24:59,560 --> 00:25:03,840
It's actually Ken Elzinga at UVA, who's the author.

118
00:25:03,840 --> 00:25:05,360
But anyway, so he has a murder mystery

119
00:25:05,360 --> 00:25:08,560
where two innocent men both confess to the crime.

120
00:25:08,560 --> 00:25:10,320
And then the economist comes along and says, well,

121
00:25:10,320 --> 00:25:12,120
of course they both confessed to the crime.

122
00:25:12,120 --> 00:25:13,720
There was a prisoner's dilemma.

123
00:25:13,720 --> 00:25:16,280
And then he goes and really solves the crime.

124
00:25:16,280 --> 00:25:18,800
There's something contrived about the murder mystery

125
00:25:18,800 --> 00:25:20,640
because then it leaves you wondering, well,

126
00:25:20,640 --> 00:25:24,520
why does anyone ever go and use this method of interrogation

127
00:25:24,520 --> 00:25:27,120
if it always leads to confessions from everybody,

128
00:25:27,120 --> 00:25:29,280
no matter whether they're innocent or guilty?

129
00:25:29,280 --> 00:25:30,360
It's kind of a dumb system.

130
00:25:33,320 --> 00:25:36,560
In the real world, there's something more to it,

131
00:25:36,560 --> 00:25:41,400
namely you would want each person, when they confess,

132
00:25:41,400 --> 00:25:45,040
to give details, facts, that they could only

133
00:25:45,040 --> 00:25:48,440
know if, in fact, they were guilty.

134
00:25:48,440 --> 00:25:51,000
So this is an important proviso if you're

135
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:52,920
using the strategy in the real world.

136
00:25:52,920 --> 00:25:56,360
Otherwise, you might get innocent confessions.

137
00:25:56,360 --> 00:26:00,200
A sub-point on the game theory of torture.

138
00:26:00,200 --> 00:26:03,440
So Roman philosopher Cicero has a very interesting discussion

139
00:26:03,440 --> 00:26:04,520
of torture.

140
00:26:04,520 --> 00:26:06,280
So in Roman times, torture is very commonly

141
00:26:06,280 --> 00:26:08,160
used to get confessions out of people.

142
00:26:08,160 --> 00:26:11,440
And what about the objection that people

143
00:26:11,440 --> 00:26:13,320
will admit to anything under torture?

144
00:26:13,320 --> 00:26:14,920
Cicero knows this one.

145
00:26:14,920 --> 00:26:18,200
And he says, yes, well, only the dumb kind of torture

146
00:26:18,200 --> 00:26:21,680
involves torturing someone until they say, I did it.

147
00:26:21,680 --> 00:26:23,840
He says that the good kind of torture,

148
00:26:23,840 --> 00:26:25,440
the kind of torture that Cicero says

149
00:26:25,440 --> 00:26:28,360
is the admirable torture, involves inflicting pain

150
00:26:28,360 --> 00:26:31,480
until someone admits facts they couldn't

151
00:26:31,480 --> 00:26:34,680
know unless they were guilty.

152
00:26:34,680 --> 00:26:37,880
All right, and that ends the set.

153
00:26:37,880 --> 00:26:39,920
So there's some logic to that, anyway.

154
00:26:39,920 --> 00:26:41,520
Yes, there is, of course, a downside.

155
00:26:41,520 --> 00:26:43,640
You might have to kill an innocent person in order

156
00:26:43,640 --> 00:26:44,360
to get this.

157
00:26:44,360 --> 00:26:47,000
All right, so.

158
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,160
I'm always puzzled by these interrogations,

159
00:26:49,160 --> 00:26:54,400
the scenarios in fiction, where someone's getting tortured,

160
00:26:54,400 --> 00:26:56,200
and then they have an answer, and they just

161
00:26:56,200 --> 00:26:57,800
don't make up something.

162
00:26:57,800 --> 00:26:59,680
So I'd always be there just making up answers,

163
00:26:59,680 --> 00:27:02,960
just to hope that Jack Bauer saves me in time,

164
00:27:02,960 --> 00:27:04,120
or something like that.

165
00:27:04,120 --> 00:27:09,080
Like, yeah, the bombs in Idaho, in Boise, in the McDonald's,

166
00:27:09,080 --> 00:27:10,760
on Main Street.

167
00:27:10,760 --> 00:27:14,360
And if that doesn't work, I'm like, oh, I messed up.

168
00:27:14,360 --> 00:27:15,200
OK.

169
00:27:15,200 --> 00:27:17,960
Anyway, when I was being tortured, I got confused.

170
00:27:17,960 --> 00:27:20,840
All right, so there is the puzzle

171
00:27:20,840 --> 00:27:24,480
of why people under duress don't make up, well,

172
00:27:24,480 --> 00:27:26,480
aren't that are liars, and don't just make up,

173
00:27:26,480 --> 00:27:29,840
and especially want to make up a very time-intensive scenario

174
00:27:29,840 --> 00:27:32,640
in order to, where they have to put in a lot of work

175
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:34,440
in order to verify what you're saying.

176
00:27:34,440 --> 00:27:37,360
Say, look, my contact can only be

177
00:27:37,360 --> 00:27:40,240
met in three months and a day.

178
00:27:40,240 --> 00:27:44,080
You have to be at the North Pole to do it.

179
00:27:44,080 --> 00:27:46,760
And until then, you're like, sorry,

180
00:27:46,760 --> 00:27:49,800
that's where it's all raised.

181
00:27:49,800 --> 00:27:51,320
Any case.

182
00:27:51,320 --> 00:27:53,560
All right, so this is the prisoner's lemma.

183
00:27:53,560 --> 00:27:56,480
That is the standard case involving the legal system.

184
00:27:56,480 --> 00:27:58,160
But it's got a lot of other applications.

185
00:27:58,160 --> 00:27:59,920
So very often, we'll bring it up when

186
00:27:59,920 --> 00:28:02,400
we're talking about public goods and externalities.

187
00:28:02,400 --> 00:28:04,080
So with a public good, what is it?

188
00:28:04,080 --> 00:28:06,080
It's in the interest of any one person to say,

189
00:28:06,080 --> 00:28:07,560
let somebody else take care of the problem.

190
00:28:07,560 --> 00:28:08,920
I'm not going to worry about it.

191
00:28:08,920 --> 00:28:10,560
But of course, if everybody says that,

192
00:28:10,560 --> 00:28:13,400
then the problem doesn't get taken care of.

193
00:28:13,400 --> 00:28:17,920
You get a very bad result. It's often used for collusion.

194
00:28:17,920 --> 00:28:21,720
So if you think about it, from a social point of view,

195
00:28:21,720 --> 00:28:24,760
is it good that we have the literal prisoner's lemma?

196
00:28:24,760 --> 00:28:27,720
If there's two people involved in a horrible crime,

197
00:28:27,720 --> 00:28:29,800
is it actually a good thing that we have a system

198
00:28:29,800 --> 00:28:33,000
to make them confess?

199
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:34,320
Sure, it's a great thing.

200
00:28:34,320 --> 00:28:36,720
So that's one where we should really

201
00:28:36,720 --> 00:28:38,640
be thinking about there being a third actor here.

202
00:28:38,640 --> 00:28:39,920
Or like everyone else, all the people

203
00:28:39,920 --> 00:28:41,760
who don't want to get murdered would be the third actor.

204
00:28:41,760 --> 00:28:43,800
And they would actually be benefiting greatly

205
00:28:43,800 --> 00:28:47,120
from a system where people in murder plots

206
00:28:47,120 --> 00:28:49,400
tend to go to that equilibrium.

207
00:28:49,400 --> 00:28:52,480
But anyway, for collusion, this is a standard story

208
00:28:52,480 --> 00:28:54,440
about oligopoly, for example.

209
00:28:54,440 --> 00:28:57,500
So in oligopoly, of course, all the firms

210
00:28:57,500 --> 00:29:00,000
that are in the industry would like to go and,

211
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,960
if they would all cooperate, all reduce production.

212
00:29:02,960 --> 00:29:05,680
It's like in OPEC, if we all go and cut back oil production

213
00:29:05,680 --> 00:29:08,160
by 20%, then based upon what we know

214
00:29:08,160 --> 00:29:09,760
about the elasticity of oil demand,

215
00:29:09,760 --> 00:29:12,720
this would increase total revenue in the oil industry.

216
00:29:12,720 --> 00:29:15,680
And then we'd all be better off.

217
00:29:15,680 --> 00:29:17,360
So often used for collusion as well,

218
00:29:17,360 --> 00:29:19,760
even though there, of course, as we know,

219
00:29:19,760 --> 00:29:23,040
the deadweight costs of collusion are there.

220
00:29:23,040 --> 00:29:24,920
But from the point of view of the firms,

221
00:29:24,920 --> 00:29:27,000
why is the firms don't go and cooperate

222
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:28,080
with each other more?

223
00:29:28,080 --> 00:29:29,920
And prisoner's lemma's a standard story

224
00:29:29,920 --> 00:29:32,960
because right after you make the collusive agreement,

225
00:29:32,960 --> 00:29:34,200
then the first thing you want to do

226
00:29:34,200 --> 00:29:36,120
is call up all your customers and say,

227
00:29:36,120 --> 00:29:37,440
look, tomorrow there's going to be an announcement

228
00:29:37,440 --> 00:29:38,920
that the price of increased,

229
00:29:38,920 --> 00:29:40,680
but for my very special customers,

230
00:29:40,680 --> 00:29:44,180
my favorite customer in the world, John,

231
00:29:44,180 --> 00:29:45,020
that doesn't apply to you.

232
00:29:45,020 --> 00:29:47,320
You keep getting the good old rates.

233
00:29:47,320 --> 00:29:49,240
And after you've called all of your customers

234
00:29:49,240 --> 00:29:51,320
to tell them that their rates aren't going up,

235
00:29:51,320 --> 00:29:53,340
then you start calling all of your competitor's

236
00:29:53,340 --> 00:29:55,280
best customers and say, I've heard a rumor

237
00:29:55,280 --> 00:29:56,960
that your rates are going to go up.

238
00:29:56,960 --> 00:29:59,000
And I would just like you to know

239
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:00,760
that you do have plenty of other options

240
00:30:00,760 --> 00:30:02,380
for your petroleum needs.

241
00:30:02,380 --> 00:30:03,480
And then you do that.

242
00:30:03,480 --> 00:30:05,120
But of course, if everybody does that,

243
00:30:05,120 --> 00:30:07,120
then nothing much happens.

244
00:30:07,120 --> 00:30:09,200
If you go and read about the history

245
00:30:09,200 --> 00:30:10,800
of cartels in the United States

246
00:30:10,800 --> 00:30:12,920
before the antitrust laws,

247
00:30:12,920 --> 00:30:15,000
it's very colorful and gets a lot,

248
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:18,320
a lot of it does involve a lot of empty promises

249
00:30:18,320 --> 00:30:19,480
to go and reduce production.

250
00:30:19,480 --> 00:30:21,240
And then the promises,

251
00:30:21,240 --> 00:30:23,620
the empty promises are broken very quickly.

252
00:30:25,320 --> 00:30:26,600
All right, so collusion.

253
00:30:26,600 --> 00:30:28,320
People often looked at voting like this.

254
00:30:28,320 --> 00:30:30,400
Is that only we could go and get,

255
00:30:30,400 --> 00:30:32,120
wouldn't it be better if everybody voted?

256
00:30:32,120 --> 00:30:34,760
That one's a little bit odd because we'll,

257
00:30:34,760 --> 00:30:38,760
what if it just kept the result the same?

258
00:30:38,760 --> 00:30:39,880
There wouldn't just be more people

259
00:30:39,880 --> 00:30:41,800
have to take time off during their busy day

260
00:30:41,800 --> 00:30:43,000
to get the same result.

261
00:30:43,960 --> 00:30:48,840
But things like if there's a small interest group

262
00:30:48,840 --> 00:30:49,880
that is well organized

263
00:30:49,880 --> 00:30:51,600
and they're taking advantage of the public,

264
00:30:51,600 --> 00:30:53,120
and then the public's getting ripped off,

265
00:30:53,120 --> 00:30:56,600
if only the members of the public would all go and vote,

266
00:30:56,600 --> 00:30:58,600
then you'd get a good result.

267
00:30:58,600 --> 00:31:01,160
So often used there.

268
00:31:01,160 --> 00:31:03,360
This is often used to understand revolution.

269
00:31:03,360 --> 00:31:05,240
So like in North Korea right now,

270
00:31:05,240 --> 00:31:07,280
like what fraction of the population would be better off

271
00:31:07,280 --> 00:31:09,480
if they could just eliminate the government

272
00:31:09,480 --> 00:31:11,480
and surrender North Korea to South Korea?

273
00:31:12,560 --> 00:31:13,400
80%.

274
00:31:13,400 --> 00:31:15,000
Yeah, yeah, very, very high percentage

275
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:17,160
would be better off if they could just go

276
00:31:17,160 --> 00:31:19,800
and peacefully surrender to South Korea.

277
00:31:19,800 --> 00:31:22,640
And just say, take us over now, please.

278
00:31:22,640 --> 00:31:26,120
All right, so that's all we ask is to take control.

279
00:31:26,120 --> 00:31:28,880
All right, but who wants to be the first person

280
00:31:28,880 --> 00:31:30,680
to stick his neck out in North Korea?

281
00:31:31,760 --> 00:31:33,600
Well, there's a lot of people

282
00:31:33,600 --> 00:31:36,320
that have possibly stuck their necks out.

283
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:38,000
It's hard to understand what's really going on,

284
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:39,680
but the people that are accused

285
00:31:39,680 --> 00:31:41,760
of sticking their necks out are dead.

286
00:31:41,760 --> 00:31:44,920
So like the uncle of the current dictator

287
00:31:44,920 --> 00:31:46,720
was merrily executed.

288
00:31:46,720 --> 00:31:49,320
There's some rumors that he was torn apart by wild dogs

289
00:31:49,320 --> 00:31:51,320
in front of the whole Politburo.

290
00:31:51,320 --> 00:31:52,680
You read a lot of people saying, no,

291
00:31:52,680 --> 00:31:54,240
that probably wasn't really true.

292
00:31:54,240 --> 00:31:55,400
It was probably just a machine gun

293
00:31:55,400 --> 00:31:56,880
with an anti-aircraft weapon.

294
00:31:56,880 --> 00:31:57,720
All right.

295
00:31:59,160 --> 00:32:01,800
And yet the Politburo didn't have to see it.

296
00:32:01,800 --> 00:32:04,400
All right, so things like that.

297
00:32:04,400 --> 00:32:06,240
There is a story which I think is confirmed

298
00:32:06,240 --> 00:32:08,640
that in the middle of the Iran-Iraq war,

299
00:32:08,640 --> 00:32:12,640
one member of Saddam Hussein's cabinet said,

300
00:32:12,640 --> 00:32:14,640
the Iranians are very upset

301
00:32:14,640 --> 00:32:17,640
with a beloved comrade, Saddam Hussein,

302
00:32:17,640 --> 00:32:20,520
so perhaps it would be easier for us to negotiate peace

303
00:32:20,520 --> 00:32:24,480
if he would just temporarily, officially,

304
00:32:24,480 --> 00:32:28,400
just as a formality,

305
00:32:28,400 --> 00:32:31,600
just step down from the leadership position.

306
00:32:31,600 --> 00:32:35,280
And supposedly Saddam Hussein just took the guy,

307
00:32:35,280 --> 00:32:36,120
