Do you remember hearing about that?

59
00:42:14,820 --> 00:42:16,180
It was just like a top-down,

60
00:42:16,180 --> 00:42:18,260
you know, hey, we're switching from the left to the right.

61
00:42:18,260 --> 00:42:21,060
So for this period of time, watch out.

62
00:42:21,060 --> 00:42:22,820
I don't understand.

63
00:42:22,820 --> 00:42:25,020
All right, now this coordination,

64
00:42:25,020 --> 00:42:28,140
the approved coordination underlies a large

65
00:42:28,140 --> 00:42:31,980
and still hot policy area.

66
00:42:31,980 --> 00:42:35,580
So this is often called the path dependence literature.

67
00:42:35,580 --> 00:42:38,140
All right, all right, and the main idea here

68
00:42:38,140 --> 00:42:40,660
is that it is possible for people to be locked

69
00:42:40,660 --> 00:42:43,340
into a Pareto inferior equilibria,

70
00:42:43,340 --> 00:42:46,580
or to be locked into a Pareto inferior equilibrium,

71
00:42:47,900 --> 00:42:50,900
usually in the context of technology.

72
00:42:50,900 --> 00:42:52,940
Usually in the context of technology.

73
00:42:52,940 --> 00:42:56,020
The idea is that there could be a bunch of technologies,

74
00:42:56,020 --> 00:42:58,140
some are going one to one to the best,

75
00:42:58,140 --> 00:43:00,500
but if we start off with the wrong technology,

76
00:43:00,500 --> 00:43:02,180
then people will just stay with that one

77
00:43:02,180 --> 00:43:04,100
because nobody wants to be the only person

78
00:43:04,100 --> 00:43:06,500
using some weird technology that nobody else uses.

79
00:43:06,500 --> 00:43:07,340
Beta-max.

80
00:43:07,340 --> 00:43:08,260
Yes, beta-max.

81
00:43:08,260 --> 00:43:10,900
So the most famous example is,

82
00:43:11,940 --> 00:43:14,100
which probably is barely,

83
00:43:14,100 --> 00:43:16,580
you'll see, you guys have watched a videotape at some point,

84
00:43:16,580 --> 00:43:18,220
have you like put a videocassette

85
00:43:18,220 --> 00:43:19,580
into a machine at some point?

86
00:43:19,580 --> 00:43:20,420
We had beta-max.

87
00:43:20,420 --> 00:43:22,180
Yes, all right.

88
00:43:22,180 --> 00:43:24,740
So anyway, back when I was a kid,

89
00:43:24,740 --> 00:43:27,180
there was a great format war between VHS,

90
00:43:27,180 --> 00:43:29,220
which won, and beta-max.

91
00:43:29,220 --> 00:43:31,020
All right, for a very long time,

92
00:43:31,020 --> 00:43:32,820
there were people saying that beta-max should have won,

93
00:43:32,820 --> 00:43:36,180
it deserved to win, and was the superior technology.

94
00:43:36,180 --> 00:43:37,860
And then there was a story saying

95
00:43:37,860 --> 00:43:39,500
that the problem was path dependence.

96
00:43:39,500 --> 00:43:41,540
VHS came on first, and then just locked in,

97
00:43:41,540 --> 00:43:44,260
and then no one wanted to do something different.

98
00:43:44,260 --> 00:43:46,700
This is a deeply factually wrong story

99
00:43:46,700 --> 00:43:48,540
because beta-max came first.

100
00:43:48,540 --> 00:43:51,100
VHS won anyway, right?

101
00:43:51,100 --> 00:43:53,460
And then you're like, wow, that's kind of weird.

102
00:43:53,460 --> 00:43:56,620
So it was worse, and it still won,

103
00:43:56,620 --> 00:43:57,740
even though it came second.

104
00:43:57,740 --> 00:43:59,540
It was worse and late, and it won.

105
00:44:00,860 --> 00:44:02,500
There's actually a pretty,

106
00:44:02,500 --> 00:44:04,980
while there were some clear advantages for beta-max,

107
00:44:04,980 --> 00:44:07,460
so it gave you better picture quality, things like that,

108
00:44:07,460 --> 00:44:10,340
but there also was a clear advantage for VHS,

109
00:44:10,340 --> 00:44:12,900
which is you could fit an entire movie on one tape.

110
00:44:13,940 --> 00:44:17,380
Now, clearly, every human being on Earth

111
00:44:17,380 --> 00:44:19,180
values picture quality over having to get up

112
00:44:19,180 --> 00:44:21,220
in the middle of a movie and switch tapes, right?

113
00:44:21,220 --> 00:44:26,220
No, actually, there's a whole unwashed,

114
00:44:26,420 --> 00:44:28,020
all the unwashed masses might sit there

115
00:44:28,020 --> 00:44:30,580
and not even care about what the picture quality looks like,

116
00:44:30,580 --> 00:44:32,100
but they don't want to have to get up out of their chair

117
00:44:32,100 --> 00:44:33,140
and change the tape.

118
00:44:33,140 --> 00:44:34,420
So that's another explanation,

119
00:44:34,420 --> 00:44:38,380
was that VHS actually was the better technology

120
00:44:38,380 --> 00:44:41,460
given the preferences of the philistines

121
00:44:41,460 --> 00:44:42,820
that inhabit a narrow town,

122
00:44:42,820 --> 00:44:46,380
or whatever story you want to tell.

123
00:44:46,380 --> 00:44:49,180
You also remember the televisions back then were so bad,

124
00:44:49,180 --> 00:44:51,180
I know it's unclear what that was a thing,

125
00:44:51,180 --> 00:44:54,220
what VCR, what kind of VCR you had,

126
00:44:54,220 --> 00:44:55,900
the televisions were terrible too.

127
00:44:57,220 --> 00:44:59,580
Any case, so, but as you can see here,

128
00:44:59,580 --> 00:45:01,580
it's possible to get locked in there.

129
00:45:01,580 --> 00:45:04,260
Now, possibility is very different from proof,

130
00:45:04,260 --> 00:45:05,860
and this is the thing to watch

131
00:45:05,860 --> 00:45:08,500
whenever you're reading empirical applications

132
00:45:08,500 --> 00:45:11,380
of this concept, because very often,

133
00:45:11,380 --> 00:45:14,380
people who don't will go and start applying the model

134
00:45:14,380 --> 00:45:16,100
before they know the facts, right?

135
00:45:16,100 --> 00:45:18,700
I think we were gonna talk about some other example,

136
00:45:18,700 --> 00:45:22,140
we'll talk about some other factual examples as we go on,

137
00:45:22,140 --> 00:45:25,580
but there's two economists that are well known at GMU,

138
00:45:25,580 --> 00:45:27,700
Margolis and Leibovitz, who have gone through

139
00:45:27,700 --> 00:45:30,540
and debunked a bunch of very popular examples

140
00:45:31,900 --> 00:45:32,980
of path dependence.

141
00:45:32,980 --> 00:45:34,260
Probably the one they're most famous for

142
00:45:34,260 --> 00:45:35,420
is the QWERTY keyboard.

143
00:45:36,420 --> 00:45:39,860
So, now, so the anyway, so you know how,

144
00:45:39,860 --> 00:45:42,420
if you just visualize typing Q,

145
00:45:42,420 --> 00:45:45,340
Q-W-E-R-T-Y on a keyboard,

146
00:45:45,340 --> 00:45:46,660
you're basically going to the second row

147
00:45:46,660 --> 00:45:50,740
and just typing letters in order from left to right.

148
00:45:50,740 --> 00:45:52,340
All right, now there's a standard story

149
00:45:52,340 --> 00:45:54,780
about why keyboards look this way,

150
00:45:54,780 --> 00:45:57,380
and the story goes back when we had regular typewriters

151
00:45:57,380 --> 00:45:59,140
with physical keys.

152
00:45:59,140 --> 00:46:01,620
Anyone ever typed on a regular physical typewriter

153
00:46:01,620 --> 00:46:02,820
with keys?

154
00:46:02,820 --> 00:46:03,980
Yes.

155
00:46:03,980 --> 00:46:06,500
So you had to actually, the goal was to,

156
00:46:06,500 --> 00:46:08,820
supposedly was to slow things down a bit,

157
00:46:08,820 --> 00:46:11,180
be to avoid jamming keys.

158
00:46:11,180 --> 00:46:14,380
All right, and then, supposedly,

159
00:46:14,380 --> 00:46:16,380
there was an Admiral Dvorsak who went and came

160
00:46:16,380 --> 00:46:18,780
with a new keyboard, which was actually better.

161
00:46:18,780 --> 00:46:19,860
And it was supposedly even better

162
00:46:19,860 --> 00:46:22,460
even with the problem of jamming of keys.

163
00:46:22,460 --> 00:46:25,020
Anyway, so, and this story's been told repeatedly,

164
00:46:25,020 --> 00:46:26,460
and then, of course, once you had computers

165
00:46:26,460 --> 00:46:28,900
and computer keyboards, those keyboards don't jam

166
00:46:28,900 --> 00:46:31,140
in the same way physical ones jam, right?

167
00:46:31,140 --> 00:46:32,860
So then it would seem like it would make a lot of sense

168
00:46:32,860 --> 00:46:35,780
to switch to a new and better keyboard configuration.

169
00:46:35,780 --> 00:46:36,740
Like when you first learn to type,

170
00:46:36,740 --> 00:46:39,900
do you ever ask someone why isn't it just alphabetical?

171
00:46:39,900 --> 00:46:42,500
That's another possible way of doing it.

172
00:46:42,500 --> 00:46:45,860
Anyway, according to Magrose Leibowitz,

173
00:46:45,860 --> 00:46:47,700
the real story is that these early experiments

174
00:46:47,700 --> 00:46:50,060
demonstrating the superiority of the Dvorsak keyboard

175
00:46:50,060 --> 00:46:52,940
were actually done by Admiral Dvorsak himself.

176
00:46:52,940 --> 00:46:54,540
And so we shouldn't put a lot of weight

177
00:46:54,540 --> 00:46:56,260
in these experiments, given that the guy

178
00:46:56,260 --> 00:46:58,020
is running and testing his own ideas.

179
00:46:58,020 --> 00:47:00,100
There was one study that was his relative, actually.

180
00:47:00,100 --> 00:47:02,020
But that was pretty much it, one study,

181
00:47:02,020 --> 00:47:03,540
and it was the guy's relative.

182
00:47:03,540 --> 00:47:05,980
Oh, his relative, and then, just through the game

183
00:47:05,980 --> 00:47:07,860
of telephone, he's repeated over and over.

184
00:47:07,860 --> 00:47:09,180
Now, here's the other interesting thing,

185
00:47:09,180 --> 00:47:11,860
is that now it is actually very easy to go

186
00:47:11,860 --> 00:47:14,020
and download new drivers so that you can transform

187
00:47:14,020 --> 00:47:17,380
a QWERTY keyboard into any keyboard you want.

188
00:47:17,380 --> 00:47:19,340
Anyone, has anyone done this?

189
00:47:19,340 --> 00:47:21,660
I've tried it once with a Dvorsak keyboard.

190
00:47:21,660 --> 00:47:23,980
And how'd it work out?

191
00:47:23,980 --> 00:47:25,500
Well, you did it only once, I guess.

192
00:47:25,500 --> 00:47:28,540
I didn't want it, like I tried for a few hours,

193
00:47:28,540 --> 00:47:29,740
and I was like, I don't want to spend the time

194
00:47:29,740 --> 00:47:31,380
to try and learn a new keyboard.

195
00:47:31,380 --> 00:47:35,220
So, I mean, so, again, of course,

196
00:47:35,220 --> 00:47:37,220
the earlier story was that nobody wants to switch

197
00:47:37,220 --> 00:47:39,620
because no one wants to have to carry around

198
00:47:39,620 --> 00:47:42,380
their own kind of physical typewriter wherever they go.

199
00:47:42,380 --> 00:47:44,860
But now, and going, once it turned out,

200
00:47:44,860 --> 00:47:46,500
you could carry around your own little keyboard,

201
00:47:46,500 --> 00:47:48,180
that's not that bad.

202
00:47:48,180 --> 00:47:49,900
And then finally, once you're in a world

203
00:47:49,900 --> 00:47:52,700
where you can just go and download the driver

204
00:47:52,700 --> 00:47:54,740
and transform any keyboard into it,

205
00:47:54,740 --> 00:47:56,220
the fact that people aren't switching

206
00:47:56,220 --> 00:47:58,620
suggests, again, it probably isn't that.

207
00:47:58,620 --> 00:48:01,740
At least it's not nearly as strong in effect

208
00:48:01,740 --> 00:48:03,500
as human-like heavy belief.

209
00:48:04,700 --> 00:48:06,180
All right.

210
00:48:06,180 --> 00:48:09,420
So, now, problems like this very naturally lead us

211
00:48:09,420 --> 00:48:14,420
to another psychologically interesting,

212
00:48:14,860 --> 00:48:16,500
the refinement of Nash equilibrium.

213
00:48:16,500 --> 00:48:18,420
This one is not really formally modeled

214
00:48:18,420 --> 00:48:19,620
because you really can't.

215
00:48:19,620 --> 00:48:21,780
It really does come down to psychology.

216
00:48:22,660 --> 00:48:25,140
But, however, a psychological idea discovered

217
00:48:25,140 --> 00:48:27,220
by the great economist Thomas Schelling,

218
00:48:27,220 --> 00:48:30,340
who just died very recently at his 90s.

219
00:48:30,340 --> 00:48:31,900
So, you know, all economists are guaranteed

220
00:48:31,900 --> 00:48:33,060
to live into their 90s.

221
00:48:33,060 --> 00:48:34,980
So, all great ones.

222
00:48:34,980 --> 00:48:35,820
All right.

223
00:48:35,820 --> 00:48:39,820
Yes, yes.

224
00:48:39,820 --> 00:48:41,860
Hmm, well, no true Scotsman.

225
00:48:41,860 --> 00:48:45,020
Yeah, he was great.

226
00:48:45,020 --> 00:48:47,580
All right, so anyway, Thomas Schelling

227
00:48:47,580 --> 00:48:49,740
looked at problems like this and then he generalized them.

228
00:48:49,740 --> 00:48:54,740
So, he came up with a very widely discussed example.

229
00:48:54,940 --> 00:48:56,820
And here's the example.

230
00:48:56,820 --> 00:48:57,980
All right, so suppose that tomorrow

231
00:48:57,980 --> 00:49:00,380
you have to meet somebody in New York City.

232
00:49:00,380 --> 00:49:02,140
You don't know the time or the place.

233
00:49:02,140 --> 00:49:04,060
Where and when do you go?

234
00:49:04,060 --> 00:49:05,300
Right, and he actually originally ran this

235
00:49:05,300 --> 00:49:06,900
as an experiment where he had everyone in his class

236
00:49:06,900 --> 00:49:09,820
write down what time you go to New York City

237
00:49:09,820 --> 00:49:12,860
and write down where you go in New York City.

238
00:49:12,860 --> 00:49:14,260
And I think over half the students

239
00:49:14,260 --> 00:49:15,980
would have actually met each other.

240
00:49:16,900 --> 00:49:20,220
All right, now what time was the standard time

241
00:49:20,220 --> 00:49:21,540
for people to go and say?

242
00:49:21,540 --> 00:49:22,380
Noon.

243
00:49:22,380 --> 00:49:25,340
Yes, noon was the most popular one.

244
00:49:25,340 --> 00:49:27,340
I think the second most popular one was midnight.

245
00:49:27,340 --> 00:49:28,620
All right, so noon or midnight.

246
00:49:28,620 --> 00:49:29,900
And then where?

247
00:49:29,900 --> 00:49:30,740
Grand Central Station.

248
00:49:30,740 --> 00:49:33,180
Yes, Grand Central Station, very popular one.

249
00:49:33,180 --> 00:49:36,340
I think the other one was the top

250
00:49:36,340 --> 00:49:37,900
of the Empire State Building.

251
00:49:37,900 --> 00:49:39,420
So, there's a movie, Sleepless in Seattle,

252
00:49:39,420 --> 00:49:40,260
where they meet at the top.

253
00:49:40,260 --> 00:49:43,820
So, I think the movie might have actually changed it.

254
00:49:43,820 --> 00:49:46,820
Anyway, so the point of this example

255
00:49:46,820 --> 00:49:50,540
is that there's an infinity of pure strategy

256
00:49:50,540 --> 00:49:52,180
in Nash Equilibria for time and place

257
00:49:52,180 --> 00:49:53,700
to meet tomorrow in New York City.

258
00:49:53,700 --> 00:49:55,260
So, all you wanna do is meet the other person.

259
00:49:55,260 --> 00:49:57,100
You just wanna be wherever they are at the time

260
00:49:57,100 --> 00:49:58,300
they're going to be there.

261
00:49:58,300 --> 00:49:59,860
So, it's just like this game except

262
00:49:59,860 --> 00:50:02,220
with infinite number of equilibria.

263
00:50:02,220 --> 00:50:04,060
Right, and if you go to anything,

264
00:50:04,060 --> 00:50:06,460
any place other than the ones on the diagonal,

265
00:50:06,460 --> 00:50:08,060
then you get nothing.

266
00:50:08,060 --> 00:50:10,820
And yet, somehow, in this incredibly,

267
00:50:10,820 --> 00:50:13,740
in an infinite universe, most people succeeded.

268
00:50:14,780 --> 00:50:17,660
Right, now, Schelling, I think, called this,

269
00:50:17,660 --> 00:50:19,700
called the idea of this the focal point.

270
00:50:21,300 --> 00:50:23,100
Right, and other people just called it the Schelling point.

271
00:50:23,100 --> 00:50:25,780
I think not only Schelling actually named it after himself,

272
00:50:25,780 --> 00:50:28,580
let's just say he wasn't that kind of a guy.

273
00:50:28,580 --> 00:50:33,500
But, the idea is that some coordination equilibria

274
00:50:33,500 --> 00:50:35,540
are more obvious than others.

275
00:50:36,660 --> 00:50:39,900
Some coordination equilibria are more obvious than others.

276
00:50:39,900 --> 00:50:42,300
And so, even though they're all equally good

277
00:50:42,300 --> 00:50:46,380
in terms of the formal definition of Nash Equilibrium,

278
00:50:46,380 --> 00:50:48,140
nevertheless, some are much more likely

279
00:50:48,140 --> 00:50:49,820
to actually get played.

280
00:50:49,820 --> 00:50:52,140
All right, now, just take a look at this game here.

281
00:50:52,140 --> 00:50:55,140
What would you say is the Schelling point in that game?

282
00:50:55,140 --> 00:50:58,460
Right, right.

283
00:50:58,460 --> 00:51:01,460
I mean, imagine if I went and did an experiment

284
00:51:01,460 --> 00:51:03,460
where you get paired up with another person,

285
00:51:03,460 --> 00:51:06,180
and then the other person plays left.

286
00:51:06,180 --> 00:51:08,780
Like, how angry would you be, this person?

287
00:51:08,780 --> 00:51:11,820
You'd say, why on earth would you play left?

288
00:51:11,820 --> 00:51:13,820
And the answer was, I only did it

289
00:51:13,820 --> 00:51:15,020
because I thought you would play left.

290
00:51:15,020 --> 00:51:17,220
Why would you think I would play left?

291
00:51:18,340 --> 00:51:20,460
I don't know, you seem like a left kind of person.

292
00:51:20,460 --> 00:51:22,620
I don't know, something like that.

293
00:51:22,620 --> 00:51:25,260
So, one thing that can make an equilibrium vocal

294
00:51:25,260 --> 00:51:26,540
is just being higher.

295
00:51:26,540 --> 00:51:30,460
So, notice, these are two equally good Nash Equilibria.

296
00:51:30,460 --> 00:51:31,980
And furthermore, they are both,

297
00:51:31,980 --> 00:51:34,380
they are both, so if we move simultaneously,

298
00:51:34,380 --> 00:51:36,420
these are both sub-game perfect.

299
00:51:36,420 --> 00:51:37,860
There's both sub-game perfect.

300
00:51:37,860 --> 00:51:41,220
So, there's nothing that is unbelievable about,

301
00:51:41,220 --> 00:51:43,940
you know, given that you know someone else is playing left,

302
00:51:43,940 --> 00:51:47,060
there's nothing hard to believe about them playing left, too.

303
00:51:48,380 --> 00:51:50,140
All right, so, like, if you knew the other person

304
00:51:50,140 --> 00:51:51,860
was gonna play left, or the other person said,

305
00:51:51,860 --> 00:51:54,820
I'm gonna play left, would you then say, no, you won't.

306
00:51:54,820 --> 00:51:57,180
Actually, like, given that the person is weird enough

307
00:51:57,180 --> 00:52:00,460
to say they're playing left, I guess they will play left.

308
00:52:00,460 --> 00:52:02,660
Which means, I guess I better play left.

309
00:52:02,660 --> 00:52:04,040
So, it's sub-game perfect.

310
00:52:05,580 --> 00:52:07,580
All right, anyway, so that's what it is.

311
00:52:09,940 --> 00:52:13,620
Now, of course, so one thing that might make things,

312
00:52:13,620 --> 00:52:16,900
that might make an equilibrium, you know, focal,

313
00:52:16,900 --> 00:52:19,580
or a showing point, is just if it's a fair payoff.

314
00:52:19,580 --> 00:52:22,220
But again, imagine that we're doing the meeting

315
00:52:22,220 --> 00:52:24,180
in New York City, and you can eat,

316
00:52:24,180 --> 00:52:27,500
and then, you know, like, you've got all these meeting ones,

317
00:52:27,500 --> 00:52:30,740
but imagine that there's, you know, there's,

318
00:52:31,700 --> 00:52:33,380
if you, as long as you meet, you get a payoff,

