205
01:08:51,400 --> 01:08:55,400
So, how does it change a game if you play it more than once?

206
01:08:55,400 --> 01:09:00,400
Alright, so, of course, it is very common that we have the same kind of interaction

207
01:09:00,400 --> 01:09:02,400
with the same people over and over again.

208
01:09:02,400 --> 01:09:08,400
So, you see your coworkers day after day after day, you see your friends, your family, fellow students.

209
01:09:08,400 --> 01:09:12,400
So, these are all cases where you're having the same kind of interaction with other people.

210
01:09:12,400 --> 01:09:15,400
So, even if it's strategic interaction, it's still strategic interaction,

211
01:09:15,400 --> 01:09:20,400
which is affected by the fact that you know you'll be doing the same thing again.

212
01:09:20,400 --> 01:09:26,400
So, if players condition their behavior in one game on what happened in a previous game, what happens?

213
01:09:26,400 --> 01:09:32,400
And the general answer is more equilibria may be sustainable.

214
01:09:32,400 --> 01:09:36,400
So, it's a very weak claim. More may.

215
01:09:36,400 --> 01:09:38,400
More equilibria may be sustainable.

216
01:09:38,400 --> 01:09:41,400
Alright, so there's two main cases that we're going to consider.

217
01:09:41,400 --> 01:09:47,400
The first one is the case of finite repetition, and the second one is of infinite repetition.

218
01:09:47,400 --> 01:10:14,400
So, we've got the case of finite repetition, and then there's the case of infinite repetition.

219
01:10:14,400 --> 01:10:21,400
Now, games that probabilistically end, but don't have any fixed upper bound on the number of turns,

220
01:10:21,400 --> 01:10:24,400
count as infinitely repeated for all purposes that I'm going to tell you.

221
01:10:24,400 --> 01:10:30,400
So, in other words, if there's a game where 90% of the time you get to play another turn,

222
01:10:30,400 --> 01:10:34,400
and every time you play there's another 90% chance you get to play another turn,

223
01:10:34,400 --> 01:10:41,400
that one counts as infinite, even though on average that game will only last 10 turns.

224
01:10:41,400 --> 01:10:47,400
So, in other words, you think about it as potentially infinite, could go on forever,

225
01:10:47,400 --> 01:10:49,400
so that counts as infinite.

226
01:10:49,400 --> 01:10:54,400
So, when you think about it that way, you realize that the space of infinitely repeated games

227
01:10:54,400 --> 01:10:56,400
is actually much bigger than it sounds at first.

228
01:10:56,400 --> 01:11:00,400
At first, like, wait, how many things have happened in an infinite number of times so far?

229
01:11:00,400 --> 01:11:03,400
As far as I know, nothing has ever happened in an infinite number of times.

230
01:11:03,400 --> 01:11:06,400
I guess this takes you into cosmology and so on,

231
01:11:06,400 --> 01:11:09,400
but it doesn't seem like things have happened in an infinite number of times.

232
01:11:09,400 --> 01:11:14,400
My friend, philosophy Michael Humer, has a whole book called Approaching Infinity on the Philosophy of Infinity.

233
01:11:14,400 --> 01:11:17,400
But anyway, it doesn't seem like it.

234
01:11:17,400 --> 01:11:19,400
So, things very rarely happen in an infinite number of times,

235
01:11:19,400 --> 01:11:23,400
but it's common that things could happen another time.

236
01:11:23,400 --> 01:11:26,400
In fact, normally, like, couldn't it happen one more time?

237
01:11:26,400 --> 01:11:28,400
Right, maybe you say, well, that's the last chance,

238
01:11:28,400 --> 01:11:33,400
but couldn't it just be one more time, just like when there's a child who wants to stay up for another minute?

239
01:11:33,400 --> 01:11:35,400
Couldn't I just stay up for one more minute?

240
01:11:35,400 --> 01:11:38,400
No, but every time they ask, like, there's a chance they'll give in.

241
01:11:38,400 --> 01:11:40,400
There's always a chance.

242
01:11:40,400 --> 01:11:44,400
So, there's a chance I could stay up the whole night, maybe one in a hundred trillion.

243
01:11:44,400 --> 01:11:50,400
But still, every time I try, I saw there's always a chance that I get another minute.

244
01:11:50,400 --> 01:11:51,400
Okay.

245
01:11:51,400 --> 01:11:53,400
Alright, so if the game's probabilistically end,

246
01:11:53,400 --> 01:11:55,400
but they don't have a fixed upper bound to the number of games,

247
01:11:55,400 --> 01:11:58,400
then they count as infinitely repeated.

248
01:11:58,400 --> 01:12:03,400
Alright, now, let's think about a case where two players first play a prisoner's limit game.

249
01:12:03,400 --> 01:12:05,400
Let's see. Yeah, right there.

250
01:12:05,400 --> 01:12:08,400
Alright, so, then they play a coordination game.

251
01:12:08,400 --> 01:12:11,400
And let's just stick with the same payoffs, because that makes life easy.

252
01:12:11,400 --> 01:12:12,400
Alright.

253
01:12:12,400 --> 01:12:18,400
Now, notice, so we play, as I said, so we play a prisoner's limit, then we play a coordination game.

254
01:12:18,400 --> 01:12:22,400
Is this finite or infinite?

255
01:12:22,400 --> 01:12:24,400
Finite.

256
01:12:24,400 --> 01:12:29,400
Finite. It is a game which is composed of two games, and two is less than infinity.

257
01:12:29,400 --> 01:12:34,400
Alright, so it's just two games, and we snap them together into one big game.

258
01:12:34,400 --> 01:12:35,400
Okay.

259
01:12:35,400 --> 01:12:40,400
Now, are the original equilibria still possible?

260
01:12:40,400 --> 01:12:42,400
And the answer is yes, always.

261
01:12:42,400 --> 01:12:47,400
So, whenever you do repetition, it always remains theoretically possible that what happens is,

262
01:12:47,400 --> 01:12:50,400
whatever was the equilibrium of the games taken separately, this happens again.

263
01:12:50,400 --> 01:12:56,400
So, we have one equilibrium where both play don't cooperate, and then both play left.

264
01:12:56,400 --> 01:12:59,400
Or they both play don't cooperate, and they both play right.

265
01:12:59,400 --> 01:13:04,400
So, those are always possibilities where we just take the equilibria in one game,

266
01:13:04,400 --> 01:13:07,400
and the equilibrium in the other game, and we just say those things happen again.

267
01:13:07,400 --> 01:13:09,400
And that's always possible.

268
01:13:09,400 --> 01:13:18,400
But, there is a Pareto superior outcome that is now possible just because we've snapped together two games.

269
01:13:18,400 --> 01:13:21,400
Alright, and this is going to seem really weird to you.

270
01:13:21,400 --> 01:13:24,400
Based upon past experience, people find this very strange.

271
01:13:24,400 --> 01:13:30,400
But, I swear to you, this is totally valid in terms of the logic of game theory.

272
01:13:30,400 --> 01:13:33,400
Alright, and here is what I propose.

273
01:13:33,400 --> 01:13:40,400
Suppose that each player plays left in the second game, if either player failed to play cooperate in the first game.

274
01:13:40,400 --> 01:13:48,400
Right, and otherwise, they both play, and otherwise they both play right.

275
01:13:48,400 --> 01:13:51,400
So, in other words, here's what I'm proposing.

276
01:13:51,400 --> 01:13:53,400
So, imagine you're playing this game with me.

277
01:13:53,400 --> 01:13:55,400
And I say, alright, this is what should happen.

278
01:13:55,400 --> 01:14:00,400
Alright, so, if either of us ever plays don't cooperate, then we both play left.

279
01:14:00,400 --> 01:14:05,400
But, if as long as we both, or as soon as, wait, did I say don't, or did I say,

280
01:14:05,400 --> 01:14:08,400
if either of us ever fails to cooperate, then we both play left.

281
01:14:08,400 --> 01:14:14,400
But, as long as both of us cooperate in turn one, then we both play right in turn two.

282
01:14:14,400 --> 01:14:17,400
Alright, now, is this a Nash equilibrium?

283
01:14:17,400 --> 01:14:19,400
Right, actually it is.

284
01:14:19,400 --> 01:14:20,400
Because, think about this.

285
01:14:20,400 --> 01:14:23,400
Now, you may be saying, oh, well this guy is a dummy.

286
01:14:23,400 --> 01:14:24,400
You know what I'm going to do?

287
01:14:24,400 --> 01:14:25,400
I'm going to say, yeah, wonderful idea.

288
01:14:25,400 --> 01:14:29,400
And then I'm going to go and play don't cooperate in turn one.

289
01:14:29,400 --> 01:14:33,400
Alright, now, as long as you stick to my plan, what do you get?

290
01:14:33,400 --> 01:14:37,400
So, you get five in turn one, and you get five in turn two for a total of ten.

291
01:14:37,400 --> 01:14:41,400
We're not doing any discounting yet, although we will do discounting as we proceed.

292
01:14:41,400 --> 01:14:43,400
Anyway, so you just figure there's no discounting.

293
01:14:43,400 --> 01:14:45,400
So, then we both get ten.

294
01:14:45,400 --> 01:14:46,400
Sounds good.

295
01:14:46,400 --> 01:14:51,400
Now, you may be thinking, ha, sucker, I'm going to go and play don't cooperate in turn one.

296
01:14:51,400 --> 01:14:53,400
And then what are you going to do about it?

297
01:14:53,400 --> 01:14:55,400
Well, let's find out what happens.

298
01:14:55,400 --> 01:14:57,400
So, I play don't cooperate in turn one, then I get six.

299
01:14:57,400 --> 01:15:03,400
And now, based upon what we said, we're both going to play left.

300
01:15:03,400 --> 01:15:06,400
Now, you may say, well, I'm not going to go along with that and play left.

301
01:15:06,400 --> 01:15:08,400
I'm going to play right.

302
01:15:08,400 --> 01:15:10,400
So, but here's the problem.

303
01:15:10,400 --> 01:15:15,400
Given that I'm playing left in turn two, what do you get when you play right in turn two?

304
01:15:15,400 --> 01:15:16,400
Zero.

305
01:15:16,400 --> 01:15:20,400
So, if you go and deviate, instead of getting ten, you get six.

306
01:15:20,400 --> 01:15:24,400
And you say, fine, fine, I'll go and stick with left in turn one and turn two.

307
01:15:24,400 --> 01:15:30,400
Then, instead of getting ten from doing it my way, you get six plus three, you get nine doing it your way.

308
01:15:30,400 --> 01:15:33,400
And you have actually hurt yourself.

309
01:15:33,400 --> 01:15:36,400
Right now, you may think there's something weird here.

310
01:15:36,400 --> 01:15:40,400
And you know, so the only weird thing here is Nash equilibrium itself.

311
01:15:40,400 --> 01:15:42,400
Well, so that may be weird.

312
01:15:42,400 --> 01:15:48,400
The idea here is, remember, in Nash equilibrium, all you can do, or when you're testing Nash equilibrium,

313
01:15:48,400 --> 01:15:53,400
the only question you get to ask is, holding constant with what the other person does,

314
01:15:53,400 --> 01:15:56,400
can I do better if I do something different?

315
01:15:56,400 --> 01:16:05,400
So, given that you know that I'm going to play left, if anyone failed to play cooperate in turn one,

316
01:16:05,400 --> 01:16:09,400
