227
01:28:56,980 --> 01:28:57,980
All right.

228
01:28:57,980 --> 01:29:01,980
And in terms of the pure logic of game theory, the answer is no.

229
01:29:01,980 --> 01:29:04,980
You cannot threaten non-cooperation.

230
01:29:04,980 --> 01:29:05,980
And that's really weird.

231
01:29:05,980 --> 01:29:06,980
Well, why not?

232
01:29:06,980 --> 01:29:07,980
Well, here's the thing.

233
01:29:07,980 --> 01:29:13,980
So if we both understand what's going on, then in the very last term, what will we both do?

234
01:29:13,980 --> 01:29:14,980
Not cooperate.

235
01:29:14,980 --> 01:29:15,980
Not cooperate.

236
01:29:15,980 --> 01:29:20,980
Now, we're in the second to last turn, and you're trying to persuade me to cooperate.

237
01:29:20,980 --> 01:29:22,980
What can you threaten?

238
01:29:22,980 --> 01:29:25,980
You could say, if you don't cooperate with me, I won't cooperate with you in the last turn.

239
01:29:25,980 --> 01:29:30,980
But the obvious response to that is, you weren't going to cooperate with me anyway.

240
01:29:30,980 --> 01:29:32,980
So what kind of a threat is that?

241
01:29:32,980 --> 01:29:36,980
Of course, the strategic thing is to say, oh, yeah, yeah, of course, no, I would never betray you.

242
01:29:36,980 --> 01:29:37,980
All right.

243
01:29:37,980 --> 01:29:39,980
And just hope they don't understand what's going on.

244
01:29:39,980 --> 01:29:41,980
All right.

245
01:29:41,980 --> 01:29:49,980
So in turn 100, you don't cooperate, which means that in turn 99, you cannot threaten to fail to cooperate later on

246
01:29:49,980 --> 01:29:54,980
because you know you're not going to cooperate later on, which means that in turn 98,

247
01:29:54,980 --> 01:29:57,980
you can't threaten to not cooperate in turn 99 or 100.

248
01:29:57,980 --> 01:30:02,980
Turn 97, you can't credibly threaten that you're not going to cooperate in 98, 99, or 100.

249
01:30:02,980 --> 01:30:04,980
And so we get unraveling.

250
01:30:04,980 --> 01:30:06,980
We're all the way back in turn 1.

251
01:30:06,980 --> 01:30:10,980
If someone says, if you betray me, then I won't cooperate with you in turn 2.

252
01:30:10,980 --> 01:30:14,980
So, yeah, well, you weren't going to cooperate in turn 2 anyway because by the logic of this,

253
01:30:14,980 --> 01:30:19,980
you wouldn't cooperate in 100, 99, 98, 97, so all the way back, never cooperate.

254
01:30:19,980 --> 01:30:20,980
Okay.

255
01:30:20,980 --> 01:30:25,980
So the idea is that since both players defect in the last turn no matter what,

256
01:30:25,980 --> 01:30:30,980
threatening to defect if your opponent fails to cooperate in the second to last game isn't any deterrent at all,

257
01:30:30,980 --> 01:30:34,980
so he will fail to cooperate then, and so on.

258
01:30:34,980 --> 01:30:39,980
And if we push the logic back all the way, then there's no cooperation ever.

259
01:30:39,980 --> 01:30:40,980
All right.

260
01:30:40,980 --> 01:30:48,980
So how does this example differ from the previous case where we do a prisoner's limit followed by coordination game?

261
01:30:48,980 --> 01:30:52,980
The answer is that here, although we only have two games, not 100,

262
01:30:52,980 --> 01:30:55,980
but here in the second game there's two equilibria.

263
01:30:55,980 --> 01:31:01,980
So we can threaten to go and put us into an inferior equilibria in order to get some punishments.

264
01:31:01,980 --> 01:31:06,980
But in this prisoner's limit one, each game only has one equilibria,

265
01:31:06,980 --> 01:31:10,980
and so there doesn't seem like there's anything you can really threaten.

